==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|satiatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|satiare||to fill full, satiate}}, from {{m|la|sat}} + {{m|la|satis||sufficient}} + {{m|la|satur||full}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/seɪʃɪˈeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-satiate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|satiat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fill]] to [[satisfaction]]; to [[satisfy]].
#: ''Nothing seemed to '''satiate''' her desire for knowledge.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[satisfy]] to [[excess]]. To fill to [[satiety]].

====Usage notes====
Used interchangeably with, and more common than, {{m|en|sate}}.<ref>“[http://blog.oup.com/2011/11/nov-gleanings/ Monthly Gleanings: November 2011]: ''Sate'' versus ''satiated''.”, ''[http://blog.oup.com/ OUPblog]''</ref>

